#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "86054cff-8fae-561d-a57e-31e573a45a91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21585" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/d8be54eddd0358742170a03b3c55e0cb94eaf2a7d59f12e1c4361a6197d29847.png)\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "63995ae4-21fa-5777-85ca-93585258b9f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21586" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "e0888bda-120f-52bb-a6f9-c5af9737139f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21588" :question/problem "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/0cf7942e618f0465ead5b98c6404a3747e505db75517725971c833c97121ab64.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/105bdbdcac5f0ff20f71a0131bf5bb3cbafe0e93ce8bc733a51c2dcb3fb8364f.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/96757a3aee3b161b2ba9887cd85f977f063807779604647dd791cf265790cabc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/658c15d2291368d0e6836ba19b90157d49dee4a510d58cddf7f85ddc9ff72a8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7fd1153-9c42-5083-972c-5532ef22ffc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164144} {:answer/id #uuid "f9428bfd-56d3-518a-b7af-700a22dbfa19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164145} {:answer/id #uuid "31d402f7-0b0f-5aea-888e-44bc41704d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164146} {:answer/id #uuid "3472e6c4-108c-598d-a8a5-ff5b63e9a565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164147} {:answer/id #uuid "53396cb3-3831-519b-b103-5d171b1be317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53396cb3-3831-519b-b103-5d171b1be317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4672ce0-28ff-5be4-8058-3ec40fce9aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa27b9e4-8a19-5583-914f-f8357a95c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331275" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x-2y \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x-3y \\\\  & =2(1)-3(-2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0530f74-bbb3-5ff9-b480-a101121e7f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357956} {:answer/id #uuid "6daf1f6b-4ce7-5c78-9c87-289a97f55167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357957} {:answer/id #uuid "627e608b-1bba-50a7-89b9-ec48baeb2c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357958} {:answer/id #uuid "1b140876-3f08-5901-8e58-9078eb5c3f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357959} {:answer/id #uuid "4b68b109-b013-5789-bf7c-0751423abe09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0530f74-bbb3-5ff9-b480-a101121e7f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357956} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b1c731c-32eb-5ca5-be25-a69ce334e19c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abd93af3-0e28-559c-8c83-70bb59f81df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332610" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(1,-1)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =5x-y \\\\  & =5(1)-(-1) \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x-2y \\\\  & =-1-2(-1) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be24466-905c-5bcc-91ff-62cde7a0b22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357963} {:answer/id #uuid "391d2adb-3803-5018-a1bd-d9f50f7aa6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357964} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaffa8f-a2e4-515e-ab66-491b33b6d03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357965} {:answer/id #uuid "5efc6ade-eee3-5f3d-bc17-23323f068098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357966} {:answer/id #uuid "70049e23-1e46-5e0c-9665-dceb31c06ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be24466-905c-5bcc-91ff-62cde7a0b22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357963} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f4be33-e40d-5bd5-9f53-a21fa5208ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72f0ec83-861a-5c13-a044-bab8e690fabf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332613" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(3,-1)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(3,-1)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-x+4y \\\\  & =-3+4(-1) \\\\  & =-3-4 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+2y \\\\  & =2(3)+2(-1) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc03b71-f24f-5a53-b579-263145c9b544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357970} {:answer/id #uuid "0c05fa17-a7bd-5eb9-9fb3-3da10f1dc5b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357971} {:answer/id #uuid "b904762f-2e4f-5d03-b1da-81d0d07867a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357972} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a5583-4d97-5391-be74-6d416b3aa2b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357973} {:answer/id #uuid "1175f720-5926-5741-bddd-56ed0683bac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-72 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c05fa17-a7bd-5eb9-9fb3-3da10f1dc5b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357971} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d6b2a-2c11-5699-8c0c-9adf8afbf626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75adf624-a2db-5ea7-93a2-7ef71176d432" :question/math-academy-id "q-332617" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(\\frac{2}{3})+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})+2(-36)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c3c24b-a716-560c-b9bc-e88b2de226ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357977} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cdeedc-a8ee-576d-b174-c471f0170ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357978} {:answer/id #uuid "517ac832-c2ea-5b80-a99b-ff93c447d0f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357979} {:answer/id #uuid "649a67d2-262a-530d-9b76-08d2be6fb6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357980} {:answer/id #uuid "37fddaa6-0114-5043-bb3a-238a651a5000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "649a67d2-262a-530d-9b76-08d2be6fb6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357980} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4e921e6-3e72-5a53-a80b-0ef58b3269e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c444c1ef-e0bd-56b0-aa59-17369145501f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332618" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(8) \\\\ 0(12)+3(8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+8 \\\\ 0+24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0b40cdf-19d1-52ae-9efa-560042647cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357984} {:answer/id #uuid "963668e9-9964-5cfa-970f-ba0092275bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357985} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2d989c-3ab6-53a7-91d6-53683ae70c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357986} {:answer/id #uuid "2cda35dc-ac33-5452-a4f0-5c2ced073a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0b40cdf-19d1-52ae-9efa-560042647cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357984} :answer-field/id #uuid "017cf9eb-aa57-5334-bbef-67103d4cc438" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b79f70-b28b-5c76-a1ab-ca06f5c2cf64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357989} {:answer/id #uuid "848e1084-fc13-52cb-9293-425